            <note corresp="#d259-f2" n="2" xml:id="d259-f2-content">
                <p>See <hi rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg320">p. 320</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d259-f3" n="3" xml:id="d259-f3-content">
                <p>See <hi rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg331">p. 331</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d259-f4" n="4" xml:id="d259-f4-content">
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">Post</hi>, <ref target="#pg471">p. 471</ref>. This
                    reference was probably changed or added later, as C.C.S. 680/2 postdated C.C.S.
                    520/6.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d259-f5" n="5" xml:id="d259-f5-content">
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">Supra</hi>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d259-f6" n="6" xml:id="d259-f6-content">
                <p>“Control of Strategic Bomber Forces in Europe Following the Establishment of
                    Allied Forces on the Continent”, September 14, 1944. In this memorandum the
                    British Chiefs of Staff accepted the enclosure to C.C.S. 520/4 subject to the
                    inclusion of two new subparagraphs on important industrial areas and attacks in
                    support of the Russian armies. These two proposed amendments were acceptable to
                    the United States Chiefs of Staff and the language proposed by the British
                    Chiefs of Staff became subparagraphs 5 <hi rendition="#italic">c</hi> and 5 <hi
                        rendition="#italic">e</hi> in the directive as approved and printed
                    here.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
